WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.589 --> 00:00:08.049
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Okay, thank you very much
for that.

00:00:08.049 --> 00:00:13.780
May we then turn to item 6 on the agenda,
which is the NHS England Corporate and NHS

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:14.780
Performance Report?

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:18.029
Karen, I think speaking on behalf on this
occasion of Richard Barker.

00:00:18.029 --> 00:00:25.680
Karen Wheeler&gt;&gt; Yes, indeed, and I will start
on the corporate side very briefly.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:33.399
As you know, we track quite carefully and
rigorously the progress of our delivery against

00:00:33.399 --> 00:00:39.910
our corporate priorities, and we will do that
with this future year’s business plan priorities

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:49.170
as well; the report reflects a January position
rather than an end-of-March position, but

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:54.500
we will be doing a stock-take at the end of
the year on how we have delivered against

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:56.110
this year’s priorities.

00:00:56.110 --> 00:01:05.080
A huge amount of progress has been made on
all ten priorities, and we will obviously

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:09.780
be building on that for this coming year.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:17.159
On the NHS performance side, as we all know
it has not been a particularly bad winter,

00:01:17.159 --> 00:01:26.200
and December performance was somewhat better
than the year before, but in January we have

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:35.360
started to see the pressure emerge, and that
has particularly impacted on the A&amp;E four

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:37.240
hour standard.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:45.429
I think it is worth noting that the trend
overall continues to be increased in terms

00:01:45.429 --> 00:01:53.579
of emergency admissions; if you compare the
January figures with last January, they look

00:01:53.579 --> 00:01:59.680
a lot higher, but that reflects a trough of
performance last January versus a peak of

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:06.279
performance this January, so the underlying
trend is a lot lower and it is only really

00:02:06.279 --> 00:02:10.610
1.4% up compared with December.

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:16.799
I think that is a testament to what Simon
said earlier, which is that a lot of work

00:02:16.799 --> 00:02:23.219
is going on at the provider level, working
with commissioners as well, to manage the

00:02:23.219 --> 00:02:31.670
demand side down, and to ensure that that
does not flow through into performance, where

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:34.129
we can manage it.

00:02:34.129 --> 00:02:40.989
On RTT, on the waiting time standard: again,
we are very close to hitting the target, which

00:02:40.989 --> 00:02:43.580
is positive.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:51.950
The regions differ, so the North and Midlands
and East were above targets, where London

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:59.280
was on 92% or thereabouts, and only South
was below target.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:07.349
Again, I think that is quite a good performance,
which we will obviously be trying to work

00:03:07.349 --> 00:03:11.750
with providers and NHS Improvement to try
to maintain.

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:16.250
I thought it was also just worth noting, I
was not going to bring out much more, but

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:24.650
worth noting the continued positive progress
on the mental health delivery targets, on

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:31.310
dementia, and access targets as well, so I
think that continues to be a more positive

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:32.310
story.

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:34.189
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Well, thank you Karen for
that report.

00:03:34.189 --> 00:03:40.299
Let me invite any comments or queries to Karen.

00:03:40.299 --> 00:03:48.370
Margaret Casely-Hayford&gt;&gt; I have a couple
of questions, Karen, please.

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:53.730
The first one is in connection with learning
disabilities.

00:03:53.730 --> 00:04:01.810
We have a very useful measure of reduction
of inpatient numbers.

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:08.420
What would be useful would be to know whether
it is possible to have a measure for the community

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:12.219
provision, the support on the care side?

00:04:12.219 --> 00:04:16.130
I do not know whether that is something that
you can answer now, or whether that is something

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:17.800
that we would need to wait for.

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:22.079
Karen Wheeler&gt;&gt; I think this is very much
a part of the plan.

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:25.520
I am sure Jane would want to respond on this.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:32.210
As part of the work on learning disabilities
to build greater community provision, we will

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:36.120
be wanting to find a way of measuring that.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:42.550
I do not think we have that metric yet, and
I am not able to easily track that measure,

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:45.510
but Jane, did you want to give more detail
on that?

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:46.670
Jane Cummings&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:51.600
As the Board knows, we have transforming care
partnerships that are in place across the

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:57.050
country, and they are combinations of commissioners,
local authority NHS, specialised commissioning,

00:04:57.050 --> 00:05:02.700
local providers, and people, patients and
carers, who are currently developing plans

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:07.290
to deliver building the right support, which
is the reduction in inpatient beds.

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:11.290
As part of that, it will be implementing the
new service model that the Board has seen

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:17.750
before, and that includes developing appropriate
community services in order to be able to

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:21.970
bring people out of hospital and stop them
going in, then subsequently the number of

00:05:21.970 --> 00:05:23.289
inpatient beds.

00:05:23.289 --> 00:05:30.240
Those plans are due 8th April I think, so
we will be able to come back with a more detailed

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:36.240
plan around each of those transforming care
partnerships, which will show what they are

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:38.889
expecting to do and the impact that will have.

00:05:38.889 --> 00:05:41.639
So we will be able to bring something back,
but we do not have it yet.

00:05:41.639 --> 00:05:43.440
Margaret Casely-Hayford&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:50.139
Then my next question is in connection with
ambulance performance, and it is really heartening

00:05:50.139 --> 00:05:58.900
to see the huge percentage that arrive within
the eight minutes, which is terrific, close

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:00.180
to 70%.

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:05.560
It does mean that 30% do not arrive within
the eight minutes.

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:12.750
This, again, might be something that you need
to come back on, but with regard to the 30%,

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:16.539
do we know how close they are to the eight
minutes?

00:06:16.539 --> 00:06:25.030
If there were a 40-minute hiatus, that would
be pretty significant, and it would be clear

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:27.440
that there would need to be something done
about that.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:31.810
It would be very useful to know quite how
close we are to hitting the eight minutes

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:34.090
with those that are not hitting that at the
moment.

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:39.289
Karen Wheeler&gt;&gt; Right, yes, so how far, at
a more granular level.

00:06:39.289 --> 00:06:40.289
Margaret Casely-Hayford&gt;&gt; Exactly.

00:06:40.289 --> 00:06:43.520
Karen Wheeler&gt;&gt; I cannot answer that, I am
afraid.

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:45.789
I do not know if anybody else can.

00:06:45.789 --> 00:06:46.789
Bruce?

00:06:46.789 --> 00:06:47.979
Bruce Keogh&gt;&gt; No, I cannot give you an exact
answer, but we can come back to you on that.

00:06:47.979 --> 00:06:50.380
My sense is, it is quite close.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:53.819
I will stop there.

00:06:53.819 --> 00:07:00.479
David Roberts&gt;&gt; I would like to push a bit
on RTT.

00:07:00.479 --> 00:07:06.699
I recognise that this is a shared endeavour
between commissioners and providers, and therefore

00:07:06.699 --> 00:07:13.300
there is a part of this story that is absolutely
about provider efficiency and provider quality,

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:24.000
but I suppose the overall trend also masks
the point that Simon highlighted about elective

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:32.550
day cases being up 4.7% but elective inpatients
down 2%.

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:39.449
I have a number of questions, one of which
is that we continue to hear stories in the

00:07:39.449 --> 00:07:49.780
press about the backlog of treatment and the
backlog of cases growing and not being recorded,

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:56.870
and secondly, whether a 2% fall in elective
inpatients is something we should be celebrating

00:07:56.870 --> 00:08:00.980
or actually something we should be really
much more concerned about, given that this

00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:05.919
is potentially storing up more problems for
2016/2017?

00:08:05.919 --> 00:08:10.479
I cannot work out in my head what is going
on here, and therefore I would appreciate

00:08:10.479 --> 00:08:11.520
some insights.

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:18.620
I do not who is best to answer it.

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:29.389
Bruce Keogh&gt;&gt; I am not sure that I have a
clear idea of the analysis behind that.

00:08:29.389 --> 00:08:35.010
Clearly, offsetting some of the inpatient
admissions with day case surgery is important,

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:41.380
and we do have a very clear drive to try and
do that, and that is also accelerated by the

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:46.330
advances in surgical technique and anaesthesia
in particular.

00:08:46.330 --> 00:08:53.280
With respect to your question about whether
this is a false reassurance that the figures

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:57.790
are giving us, I think we would need to come
back, but my sense is it is a bit of both.

00:08:57.790 --> 00:09:03.330
Simon Stevens: Yes, it is, as Bruce says,
a bit of both.

00:09:03.330 --> 00:09:07.980
Actually, the trend to have increases in day
case elective admissions and decreases in

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:13.420
inpatient elective admissions is a multi-year
trend, and that reflects efficiencies, as

00:09:13.420 --> 00:09:17.520
care that previously required a hospital stay
is now being done on a day-case basis, so

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:18.940
we are seeing that continuing.

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:23.030
In many ways, that is a good thing; you can
have your gall bladder out on a day case surgery

00:09:23.030 --> 00:09:25.670
without having an overnight stay in hospital,
for example.

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:27.230
Most people would welcome that.

00:09:27.230 --> 00:09:32.200
However, that trend is also masking something
else that happened during the course of 2015/2016,

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:39.730
which is not welcome, which is that for some
specialities, particularly some of the more

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:45.220
complex operations in general surgery and
orthopaedics, actually the capacity to deliver

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:47.630
that was not available.

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:54.200
So as a result, we are building up a bit of
a bulge that will then spill over into pressures

00:09:54.200 --> 00:10:01.620
on the RTT waiting times targets in 2016/2017,
and that is something that, as we come to

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:07.580
the end of the commissioning round that Paul
will discuss, we are having to work through

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:16.040
on a CCG locality by-locality basis in order
to be able to try and get RTT to where we

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:18.230
want it to be during the course of 2016/2017.

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:22.390
As Karen said, there are differences by speciality,
there are differences by geography, particularly

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:29.770
in the south of the country, and there are
very localised differences as well, so it

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:34.440
is pretty important that when a CCG says,
‘These are the volumes that I need to procure

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:41.730
in order to get my people fast access to planned
care,’ that is then matched by good capacity-planning

00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:47.590
on the part of local hospitals to be able
to ensure that that can be fulfilled, and

00:10:47.590 --> 00:10:53.840
to the extent it looks as if it cannot, then
there needs to be an upfront discussion about

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:57.370
where patients might be offered alternatives
to enable them to get timely care.

00:10:57.370 --> 00:11:06.710
David Roberts: The point about press comments
about patients with long waiting being masked,

00:11:06.710 --> 00:11:11.392
not being recorded, and actually, the problem
is a lot bigger than we think it is; what

00:11:11.392 --> 00:11:13.820
is our view on that?

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:15.450
Simon Stevens&gt;&gt; That is right.

00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:23.080
I think there are nine trusts that are currently
not reporting, and I think that, particularly

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:32.240
in London, is artificially masking a set of
pressures across London, and so we do need

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:35.420
that sorting out, and NHS Improvement are
working closely with those trusts on that.

00:11:35.420 --> 00:11:42.830
Michelle Mitchell&gt;&gt; I am just looking at the
risk register, and particularly looking at

00:11:42.830 --> 00:11:43.830
point 9.

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:49.120
Could you talk me through the progress that
has been made on specialised services, which

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:56.500
has enabled the risk to move from amber-red
to amber?

00:11:56.500 --> 00:12:06.530
Karen Wheeler&gt;&gt; We have had a number of mitigations
in place around the specialised move.

00:12:06.530 --> 00:12:18.960
They have been doing a series of reviews of
particular specialised services 

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:32.600
and activities, and are agreeing plans for
how the work can transition to new models

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:39.620
of care, focusing on QIPP, meaning efficiency
and delivery, so how we can ensure that there

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:45.360
is reduced cost and so on associated with
those, and there has been a financial recovery

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:47.250
plan, particularly in the South region as
well.

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:52.200
There have been a number of mitigations associated
with this.

00:12:52.200 --> 00:13:00.850
I think if we were assessing this now, the
date where we in January thought we might

00:13:00.850 --> 00:13:08.930
be getting to moving to amber by March, I
suspect that date is a bit further out now

00:13:08.930 --> 00:13:17.680
than we would currently assess, so this has
just slipped in terms of hitting an amber;

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:19.980
I think we are still at amber-red at this
stage.

00:13:19.980 --> 00:13:25.060
Paul Baumann&gt;&gt; I think it is worth just saying
that the predominant risk that we were looking

00:13:25.060 --> 00:13:30.830
at here was in-year financial performance,
and to an extent longer-term financial performance.

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:34.310
It is noticeable that this year the financial
performance of specialised commissioning has

00:13:34.310 --> 00:13:39.800
been transformed from the biggest problem
that we have had, frankly, in any of the previous

00:13:39.800 --> 00:13:43.430
years, to being one of our smallest problems,
if I leave aside the Cancer Drugs Fund, which

00:13:43.430 --> 00:13:47.990
is covered by a separate risk, and which we
dealt with last time.

00:13:47.990 --> 00:13:53.050
If you look at the performance of specialised
commissioning, I could just give you two statistics:

00:13:53.050 --> 00:13:57.800
in the forecast at month ten, we have a £7
million or £8 million – I forget the exact

00:13:57.800 --> 00:14:02.840
number, I think it is £8 million – variance
on a budget of about £15 billion in specialised

00:14:02.840 --> 00:14:03.840
commissioning.

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:09.030
That is light years different from the sorts
of variation, volatility and, frankly, overspend

00:14:09.030 --> 00:14:11.660
that we have had in previous years.

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:16.880
The second statistic is in the pack transparently
that you have for the next item, which is

00:14:16.880 --> 00:14:24.170
the QIPP delivery in year in 2015/2016, which
is running at 97% of what was viewed at the

00:14:24.170 --> 00:14:26.290
beginning of the year as a very ambitious
QIPP plan.

00:14:26.290 --> 00:14:31.110
Again, I would just point to that as frankly
a success of the specialised commissioning

00:14:31.110 --> 00:14:34.480
team, who have got a grip on that in-year.

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:38.070
That is by no means to say that there is not
a big challenge going forward, of course,

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:42.180
with some of the things that we have talked
about in previous meetings, the drugs pressures

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:48.070
that are coming through, and activity growth
and trying to reduce the amount of money which

00:14:48.070 --> 00:14:53.760
is being moved into specialised commissioning
on a year-by-year basis to make way for some

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:56.780
of the other strategic things we are trying
to invest in.

00:14:56.780 --> 00:15:01.820
I think in the short term medium term, the
grip is really what is reflected in that.

00:15:01.820 --> 00:15:06.570
I do not think specialised commissioning will
ever be green; I cannot conceive of a circumstance

00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:11.790
in which we would say it is completely devoid
of significant risk, but it does seem to me

00:15:11.790 --> 00:15:13.980
amber is entirely justified at the moment.

00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:21.080
Michelle Mitchell: In terms of variability
of quality, as well?

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:25.000
Paul Baumann&gt;&gt; That is not the risk that this
was designed to cover.

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:28.570
It is a discussion that we can have, but I
do not think there is any particular reason

00:15:28.570 --> 00:15:33.230
to believe that quality in specialised services
is a particular challenge.

00:15:33.230 --> 00:15:38.180
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Thank you very much, Karen,
for that report.

00:15:38.180 --> 00:15:44.250
I invite the Board to note the report and
the assurance that it gives us as to our handling

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:46.980
of corporate and NHS performance.

00:15:46.980 --> 00:15:47.980
Thank you very much indeed.

00:15:47.980 --> 00:15:48.180
We move on now, Paul, to the NHS England Finance
Report, month 10.

